# Pickups as a layer: how to do it correctly

## Status

Reverted. Pickups are whole-tile map cells `6`/`7` again, which is the state
that works. `e8e6146884e080dd`.

Decorations DO have a working 28×28 layer. So the pattern is proven — my
execution of it for pickups was not.

## What I did, and why it failed

I built the whole feature in one pass across five places:

1. layer state
2. paint interception
3. map rendering
4. generator scan
5. autosave

Then a migration effect on top. Nothing was verified between steps. When it
didn't work I debugged the consequences instead of reverting, and made it worse
twice:

- **A destructive migration.** It cleared `6`/`7` from `mapRows` — and it ran
  before the async storage restore, so it migrated the DEFAULT map, stripped
  it, and the restore then overwrote the result. It should never have deleted
  anything before the thing it migrated to was proven to render.
- **A lost paint handler.** A later edit removed the block that wrote the
  layer. Every other piece stayed in place around a hole, so nothing ever
  populated it — and I spent three rounds diagnosing rendering.

Decorations were built incrementally over a previous session and worked.
Same feature, two approaches, opposite outcomes.

## Questions

1. **Is the incremental order below right?** My instinct is that each step must
   be independently visible before the next is written:
   - layer state + render it, with a temporary hardcoded test value, so the
     drawing is proven before anything writes to it
   - paint into the layer, map cell untouched, so both representations exist
     and the old one still works
   - generator reads the layer, with the map scan kept as a fallback
   - only then, if ever, stop writing the map cell
   - migration last, and additive — never clearing the source

2. **Should the migration exist at all?** The generator can de-duplicate a
   legacy cell against a layer entry indefinitely. A permanent compatibility
   read may be better than a one-time destructive conversion, given this data
   lives in browser storage the user cannot easily back up.

3. **Is there a way to make the async-restore race structurally impossible**
   rather than gated? Everything keyed on `mapRows` runs first against
   `DEFAULT_MAP` and then again after the restore lands. That trap will catch
   the next feature too.

4. **Was quarter-tile placement even worth it for pickups?** Decorations
   benefit — several torches on one wall. A health pack and an ammo box in the
   same tile may not be a real need, and the whole feature may have been
   solving a problem that did not exist.

## What I would want to keep from the attempt

- the per-type colour palette, which made decorations far more readable
- the picker, which replaced click-to-cycle
- the console warning when placements exceed `MAX_PICKUPS`

These could be applied to whole-tile pickups without a layer at all: more than
two pickup TYPES, each with its own colour and a picker, still one per tile.
That may be most of the value for a fraction of the risk.

## Constraints for whoever does this

- decorations already work — copy their pattern exactly rather than improvising
- `MAX_PICKUPS` caps what reaches the BASIC; each emits a block
- storage restore is async and lands after first render
- never write a migration that deletes the source representation
